      "completion_tokens": 1100,
      "output": "**Water** is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance that is the main constituent of Earth's hydrosphere and the fluids of all known living organisms. It is essential for all known forms of life.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is from chemical, physical, biological, and planetary perspectives:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Chemical Identity\n*   **Molecular Formula:** **H₂O** (two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom).\n*   **Molecular Structure:** The molecule is **bent (V-shaped)** at an angle of ~104.5°. Because oxygen is more electronegative than hydrogen, the molecule is **polar**—it has a partial negative charge near the oxygen and partial positive charges near the hydrogens.\n*   **Hydrogen Bonding:** This polarity allows water molecules to stick to each other (cohesion) and to other surfaces (adhesion) via **hydrogen bonds**. This relatively weak but abundant bonding is the secret behind almost all of water’s unique properties.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Unique Physical Properties (Caused by Hydrogen Bonding)\nWater behaves differently than almost any other substance on Earth:\n\n| Property | Description | Why It Matters |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Universal Solvent** | Dissolves more substances than any other liquid. | Transports nutrients in blood, shapes geology (caves, canyons), enables cellular chemistry. |\n| **Density Anomaly** | **Ice floats.** Water is densest at 4°C (39°F); it expands when freezing. | Insulates lakes/oceans from the top down, allowing life to survive underneath frozen surfaces. |\n| **High Specific Heat Capacity** | Absorbs a huge amount of heat before temperature rises. | Stabilizes Earth’s climate (oceans act as heat batteries); stabilizes body temperature in organisms. |\n| **High Heat of Vaporization** | Takes massive energy to turn liquid water into vapor. | Sweating/evaporation cools organisms efficiently; drives the global water cycle (weather). |\n| **Surface Tension** | High cohesion creates a \"skin\" on the surface. | Allows insects to walk on water; helps pull water up plant roots (capillary action). |\n| **Three States at Earth Temps** | Exists naturally as **solid (ice), liquid (water), and gas (vapor)** within Earth's normal temperature range. | Drives the **Hydrological Cycle** (evaporation, condensation, precipitation). |\n\n---\n\n### 3. Biological Role: The \"Matrix of Life\"\n*   **Solvent for Metabolism:** Dissolves ions, proteins, DNA, and sugars, allowing chemical reactions (metabolism) to occur.\n*   **Transport Medium:** Blood (mostly water) carries oxygen, hormones, and nutrients; sap does the same in plants.\n*   **Structural Support:** Turgor pressure (water pressure inside cells) keeps plants upright and gives shape to animal cells.\n*   **Reactant:** Essential for **hydrolysis** (breaking down food) and **photosynthesis** (plants turning CO₂ + H₂O into sugar + O₂).\n*   **Temperature Buffer:** High heat capacity prevents organisms from overheating or freezing rapidly.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Planetary Distribution (The Hydrosphere)\n*   **~71% of Earth's surface** is covered by water.\n*   **97.5% is Saline** (oceans).\n*   **2.5% is Freshwater**, but most of that is locked in:\n    *   Ice caps, glaciers, and permanent snow (~68.7% of freshwater).\n    *   Groundwater (~30.1%).\n    *   **< 1% of total freshwater** (approx 0.007% of *all* water) is easily accessible in lakes, rivers, and reservoirs for human use.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Water and Human Civilization\n*   **Agriculture:** ~70% of global freshwater withdrawals go to irrigation.\n*   **Industry:** Cooling, processing, cleaning, manufacturing (e.g., ~10,000 liters to make 1 kg of cotton/jeans).\n*   **Energy:** Hydroelectric power; cooling for thermal/nuclear plants.\n*   **Sanitation/Health:** Access to clean water (SDG 6) is the single biggest factor in reducing infectious disease and child mortality.\n\n---\n\n### Summary Definition\n> **Water is a small, polar molecule (H₂O) whose hydrogen bonding creates anomalous physical properties—specifically high heat capacity, surface tension, and a solid phase less dense than its liquid phase. These properties make it the universal biological solvent, the primary driver of Earth's climate and geology, and the non-negotiable requirement for life as we know it.**"
